# Mesa's two-GPU hook, labelled here rather than in the vendor policy because
# AOSP requires vendor property_contexts to use only vendor-prefixed names:
#     vendor's and odm's property_contexts MUST use ONLY vendor-prefixed properties
# and this name is Mesa's, not ours.
#
# platform_android.c:1114 reads drm.gpu.vendor_name and, when it is set, uses
# ONLY a DRM device whose driver matches (droid_filter_device). Without it EGL
# takes the first render node it can probe, which on a two-GPU machine is not
# necessarily the card that owns the screen -- and rendering on one card while
# gralloc and the compositor use another is a blank screen with 2448 EBUSY
# commits and nothing obviously wrong in any single component.
#
# pc_select_egl.sh derives the value from whichever card has a connected
# connector, so this is a runtime fact rather than board configuration.
drm.gpu.vendor_name    u:object_r:vendor_pc_gpu_prop:s0 exact string

# The same, for the GPU that owns the DISPLAY rather than the one apps render
# on. Under PRIME offload those differ, and drm.gpu.vendor_name names the
# offload card -- which, being a single global property, dragged SurfaceFlinger
# onto the discrete GPU along with the apps and cost most of the frame rate
# (500 fish in a window: 17 fps compositing on the dGPU, 56 on the display GPU,
# with SurfaceFlinger client-compositing every frame in both cases).
#
# surfaceflinger.rc sets MESA_DRM_ROLE=display, and platform_android.c reads
# this property instead when it sees that.
#
# Deliberately the SAME label as drm.gpu.vendor_name: vendor_pc_gpu_prop
# already carries set_prop(vendor_shell) -- the domain pc_select_egl.sh runs in
# -- and get_prop(domain, ...) in property.te, so this needs no new rule to
# work, and it cannot drift out of sync with the property it shadows.
#
# Declaring it is not optional. An undeclared property takes the default label,
# which vendor_shell may not set, so under enforcing the setprop would be
# refused, the property would never appear, and Mesa's fallback would quietly
# put the compositor back on the offload GPU -- the slow path, with nothing
# failing and nothing to see. Every GRUB entry currently boots permissive,
# which is exactly why that would have gone unnoticed.
drm.gpu.display_vendor_name    u:object_r:vendor_pc_gpu_prop:s0 exact string
